  "arcf_answer": "To explain how pnpm's content-addressable store avoids package duplication, we can update the `README.md` file to include this explanation. Below is a unified diff that includes this addition to the README, explaining the mechanism by which pnpm achieves efficient package storage and avoids duplication through its content-addressable file system.\n\n```diff\n--- README.md\n+++ README.md\n@@ -20,6 +20,17 @@\n-## Getting Started\n+## How pnpm Avoids Package Duplication\n \n+pnpm uses a content-addressable store to avoid package duplication. Here's how it works:\n+\n+1. **Single Storage**: All package files are stored in a central location called the store. This means that if multiple projects depend on the same package, only a single copy of that package's files is stored on disk.\n+\n+2. **Content-Addressable Storage**: Packages are stored in the store based on their content hash. If two packages have the same content (even if they have different versions), they will be assigned the same storage location. This means that when you update a package and only one of its files changes, pnpm only adds that changed file to the store.\n+\n+3. **Links Instead of Copies**: When installing packages, instead of copying files from the store to `node_modules`, pnpm creates hard links or symlinks to the files stored in the central store. This way, multiple projects can share the same files without consuming additional disk space.\n+\n+As a result, using pnpm not only saves disk space but also speeds up installation times since files do not need to be copied repeatedly.\n \n ## Getting Started\n```\n\nThis diff introduces a new section explaining how the content-addressable store works within pnpm, detailing the key principles leading to reduced duplication and increased efficiency in package management."
